MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m11",
  "summary": "Understand vehicle lighting, body electrical circuits, instruments and accessories, read wiring diagrams, and repair wiring correctly and safely.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe halogen, HID and LED headlamps, handle bulbs correctly and explain headlamp aim.","Explain how a headlamp circuit with a relay, the indicator flasher and brake lights work, and find a failed lamp.","Read a wiring diagram: symbols, wire colours, connectors and earth points, and use it to plan tests.","Describe power windows, central locking, wipers, horn and other body circuits and their typical faults.","Explain gauges, warning lamps and senders, and fit an accessory correctly with a fuse.","Repair a wire, connector or harness so that it is safe, sealed and neat."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Never touch a halogen bulb glass with bare fingers: skin oil makes a hot spot and the bulb fails early.","HID headlamps use very high voltage. Do not touch the ballast or bulb leads with the lamps on.","A headlamp relay lets a thin switch wire control the heavy headlamp current.","Indicators flashing fast usually means a bulb is out (on thermal flasher systems).","A wiring diagram shows how the circuit is connected, not where parts are on the vehicle.","Use the diagram to choose test points: power, earth and signal at each connector.","Fuse for an added accessory: about 125% of the load current, in the wire as close to the battery or source as possible.","Never fit a fuse or wire that is too small or too large for the circuit.","Repair wires with a crimp or solder, adhesive heat-shrink and a clip to stop movement."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m11-l1", "title": "Vehicle lighting", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Lights allow you to see and be seen, so they are a safety and legal item. This lesson covers the lamp types and checks that every technician needs."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Headlamp types"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Halogen:** a tungsten filament inside a bulb filled with halogen gas. Cheap, easy to replace, and gets very hot.","**HID (xenon):** a gas-discharge lamp. An electric arc lights the gas. It needs a **ballast** to make the starting voltage, which can be over 20,000 V, so work on it only with the lamps off and the battery disconnected.","**LED:** light-emitting diodes. They last very long, use little power and need a driver circuit and cooling. Many are sealed units that are replaced whole."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Handling bulbs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Switch the lights off and let the bulb cool. **Do not touch the glass of a halogen bulb with bare fingers.** Oil from your skin makes a hot spot on the glass and the bulb fails early. Hold it by the base or use a clean cloth. Use the correct type and wattage. A higher wattage bulb can melt the lamp housing and overload the wiring."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dipped and main beam"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Dipped (low) beam** lights the road ahead without dazzling other drivers. **Main (high) beam** gives a longer, brighter beam for use when no other traffic is near. Some lamps do both with a dual filament bulb or a movable shield. A warning lamp tells the driver that main beam is on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Aim and alignment"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Headlamps must be aimed correctly. If they point too high they dazzle others, and if too low they do not show the road. To check, park the unloaded vehicle on level ground facing a wall or aiming screen at a set distance, and compare the beam cut-off line with the marks given in the manual or by the local regulation. Adjust with the screws on the lamp. Many vehicles have a levelling control, for heavy loads."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other lamps"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Side (position) lamps and tail lamps:** show the width and position of the vehicle at night.","**Brake lamps:** come on when the brake pedal is pressed.","**Indicators and hazard lamps:** flash to show direction or danger.","**Fog lamps and reversing lamps:** for poor visibility and to warn others when reversing."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Colours and legal requirements"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Lamp","Colour","Typical rule"],"rows":[["Headlamps","White or selective yellow","Dipped beam is required at night. Main beam must be switched off for oncoming traffic."],["Front position (side) lamps","White","Show the width of the vehicle at night"],["Rear position (tail) lamps","Red","Both must work. They must not be brighter than the brake lamps."],["Brake lamps","Red","Must be clearly brighter than the tail lamps. Many vehicles also have a third brake lamp."],["Indicators","Amber (or red at the rear in some regions)","Must flash at a steady rate between about 60 and 120 times a minute"],["Reversing lamps","White","Come on only when reverse is selected"],["Rear fog lamp","Red","Only for poor visibility. Usually one lamp on the driver's side of the vehicle."],["Number plate lamp","White","Must light the plate so it can be read"]],"caption":"Local laws differ, so check the rules where the vehicle is registered."},
      {"t":"p","text":"During an inspection, ask someone to stand behind the vehicle while you operate each lamp in turn, or use a reflective surface. Check each bulb for correct operation, the lens for cracks and moisture, and the housing for secure mounting. A lamp with a cracked red lens that shows white light, or a lamp with a missing bulb, is a legal defect."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Replace bulbs in pairs. Two bulbs of the same age will fail at about the same time, and the light will match from side to side."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A lit halogen bulb can burn skin and ignite fabric. HID and some LED drivers carry dangerous voltages. Always switch off and disconnect the power before you remove a lamp."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l2", "title": "Lamp types, beam patterns and headlamp aiming", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Lights decide how far a driver can see and how well others can see the driver. A badly aimed or wrongly fitted lamp is a safety fault and an offence. This lesson looks at the lamp types, the codes you will read on bulbs, the way beams are shaped and how to aim a headlamp correctly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bulb types and codes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Code","Type","Typical use","Typical power"],"rows":[["H4","Dual-filament halogen","Older headlamps, dipped and main beam in one bulb","60/55 W"],["H7","Single-filament halogen","Dipped or main beam, in separate lamps","55 W"],["H11","Single-filament halogen","Dipped beam and fog lamps","55 W"],["HB3 (9005)","Halogen","Main beam","60 W"],["P21W","Single-contact bayonet","Brake, reverse and some indicators","21 W"],["PY21W","Amber bayonet","Indicators","21 W"],["P21/5W","Dual-filament bayonet","Tail and brake in one bulb","21 W and 5 W"],["W5W","Wedge","Side lamps and number plate","5 W"],["C5W","Festoon","Interior and number plate","5 W"]],"caption":"Codes are standard, but the base and size must match the lamp. Check the manual or the old bulb."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Light output"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Technology","Typical output per lamp","Colour","Life"],"rows":[["Halogen","About 1,000 to 1,500 lumens","Warm white, about 3,200 K","About 500 to 1,000 hours"],["HID (xenon)","About 3,000 lumens","Whiter, 4,300 to 6,000 K","About 2,000 to 3,000 hours"],["LED","About 1,500 to 4,000 lumens or more","White, 5,000 to 6,500 K","10,000 hours or more"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a beam is shaped"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A dipped beam has a sharp **cut-off line**: the light below is bright and the light above is dark, so that the road is lit without dazzling oncoming drivers. The cut-off is made by a shield in a **projector** lamp, or by the shape of the reflector and the filament position in a **reflector** lamp. In countries that drive on the left, the cut-off rises to the left, to light the kerb, and in countries that drive on the right it rises to the right. A vehicle imported from the other kind of country has headlamps that dazzle oncoming traffic, so they must be changed to the right pattern or deflected with an approved method."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Retrofits and problems"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Fitting LED or HID bulbs into a halogen reflector lamp scatters the light and dazzles other drivers, because the light source is not at the point the reflector is designed for. It is illegal in many countries unless the lamp is approved for it.","Cheap bulbs may produce less light, a poor cut-off and radio interference.","Condensation inside a lamp shows a failed seal or vent. Cracked lenses and discoloured plastic reduce output.","Yellowed or hazy plastic lenses can often be restored by wet sanding and polishing, followed by a UV-resistant coating, but a badly damaged lamp needs replacing."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Self-levelling and adaptive lamps"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A loaded boot tips the vehicle back and raises the beam. Manual levelling systems let the driver lower the beam with a switch. Automatic systems use ride-height sensors and small motors to level the beam. Adaptive lamps swivel with the steering and adjust the beam pattern to speed or weather, using cameras and motors. After repairs to the suspension, or after fitting a lamp, these systems need to be calibrated with a scan tool."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Aiming a headlamp"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Park the vehicle, unloaded and with normal tyre pressures, on level ground square to a wall or screen at a set distance, often 10 m, as the maker specifies.","Mark the centre height of each lamp on the screen. Mark the vertical centre line of each lamp.","Switch on the dipped beam. Cover the other lamp.","Check that the cut-off line is at the required height and slope. Adjust the screws, usually one for vertical and one for horizontal aim.","Repeat for the other side, and check the main beam if the manual requires it."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Where should the cut-off be?","steps":["The manual gives a downward slope of 1.0% for the dipped beam, and the screen is 10 m from the lamps.","1.0% of 10 m is 0.10 m, or 10 cm. The cut-off line should therefore be 10 cm below the lamp's centre height.","If the lamp centre is 65 cm above the floor, the cut-off line should be at 65 − 10 = 55 cm.","A cut-off at 70 cm would be too high and dazzle other drivers. Use the maker's figure, as the slope varies."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Aim lamps with the correct load and tyre pressure. After any suspension, bumper or lamp repair, aim them again."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"HID ballasts and some LED drivers produce high voltage. Switch off, disconnect the battery and wait a few minutes before you touch the connectors."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l3", "title": "Lighting circuits and relays", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Headlamps need a heavy current, so they are switched with relays. This lesson shows how a lamp circuit is built and how to find a fault in one."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"headlamp-relay-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The headlamp circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The driver's switch carries only a small current to the **relay coil**. When the coil is energised, the relay contacts close, and the full lamp current flows from the battery, through a **fuse**, the relay contacts and then to the headlamps and the earth. Each headlamp is usually on a separate fuse so one failure does not leave you without any light. A dipped and main beam circuit uses two relays or a switching relay."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Daytime running lights"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Daytime running lamps come on when the engine is running. The circuit uses the ignition or the ECU and a relay. Many are LEDs and can be dimmed. On some vehicles the lamps switch off when the side lights or headlamps come on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Indicators and the flasher"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **flasher unit** switches the indicator lamps on and off about 60 to 120 times per minute. On a thermal flasher, a bulb failure reduces the current and the flash rate speeds up, which tells the driver that a lamp has failed. Electronic flashers and body computers behave in different ways: some flash fast with a missing bulb and some do not. LED lamps may need a resistor or a compatible unit to flash at the right rate."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why LED bulbs make indicators flash too fast","steps":["A conventional indicator bulb is 21 W. On 12 V it draws 21 ÷ 12 = 1.75 A. A thermal flasher is designed for the load of two or three such bulbs. A missing bulb lowers the current and tells the flasher to speed up, to warn the driver.","An LED replacement may be only 2 W. It draws 2 ÷ 12 = 0.17 A, so the flasher thinks the bulb has failed and flashes quickly. This is called hyper-flash.","A **load resistor** adds the missing current to fool the flasher. To add 1.58 A it needs R = 12 ÷ 1.58 = 7.6 Ω, and it dissipates 12 × 1.58 = 19 W as heat. It must be mounted on metal, away from plastic and wires, because it gets very hot.","A better solution is an electronic flasher or a body module that is designed for LED loads."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake lamp switch"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **brake switch** near the pedal closes when the pedal moves, supplying power to the brake lamps. It also sends a signal to the ECU, cruise control and stability systems. If the lamps stay on, the switch may be out of adjustment, and the battery will go flat."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking for a failed lamp"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the bulb and its holder for corrosion and a burned filament.","Check the fuse and the relay.","Measure the voltage at the lamp connector with the lights switched on.","If power is present, test the earth with a voltage-drop test or a continuity check.","If no power is present, work back along the wire toward the relay and fuse."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Lamp failure monitoring and CAN bus systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern cars watch each lamp circuit and show a warning if a bulb fails. A body control module measures the lamp current, or the voltage across a small resistor, and compares it with the expected value. If you fit a bulb of a different power or an LED, the module may report a failure, so use the correct part. On some vehicles, a bulb is checked when the lights are first switched on, and a message tells the driver which lamp is out. Check the scan tool when a warning shows and the bulb looks good, since a corroded socket or a faulty module is a possible cause."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Most lighting faults are a bad bulb, a poor earth or a corroded connector. Check these before you look for something more complicated."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If both lamps on one side fail, suspect a shared earth, fuse or connector. If one lamp fails, suspect the bulb or its socket."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l4", "title": "Reading wiring diagrams", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A wiring diagram is a map of a circuit. Learning to read it is one of the most valuable skills for electrical diagnosis. This lesson shows how to use one."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"wiring-diagram-reading"},
      {"t":"h","text":"What it shows"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wiring diagram shows the **electrical connections** between components, not where they are on the vehicle. Components are drawn as standard symbols, and lines show wires, labelled with a colour code and a circuit number. Lines that cross are not connected unless there is a dot at the crossing. Different makers use different styles, but the basics are the same."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Symbols and colour codes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","How it is shown"],"rows":[["Battery","Alternate long and short lines"],["Fuse","A small rectangle or an S-shape in the line"],["Switch","A break in the line with a pivoting contact"],["Relay","A box with a coil symbol and switch contact"],["Lamp","A circle with a cross or a filament in it"],["Earth (ground)","A symbol with three descending lines or a line to a labelled earth point"]],"caption":"Common symbols"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Wire colours are shown as letters, for example BK for black, RD for red, GN for green, and a stripe colour after it, for example RD/WH for red with a white stripe. The key is in the manual. Never rely on colours alone, because they can fade, and repairs may use different colours."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Connectors and pins"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Connectors are shown with a number and the pin numbers for each wire. The connector view shows which way you are looking at it, from the wire side or the terminal side. Take care to read the right view when you test."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power and earth"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Follow the diagram from the power source, through fuses and switches, to the load and then to the earth. The diagram shows the main earth points, and many circuits share one. A bad shared earth can affect several circuits."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using a diagram to plan tests"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Find the circuit for the faulty component and read through it.","Mark where you should see power, signal and earth when the circuit is working.","Choose the first test point at the middle of the circuit (half-split).","Test and write down each result against what you expect.","Follow the difference to the fault."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A worked example: a horn circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A horn circuit on a car is drawn as follows. A fuse in the underbonnet fuse box feeds terminal 30 of the horn relay. The relay coil terminal 86 is fed from the ignition-switched fuse, and terminal 85 goes to the horn button in the steering wheel, which connects to earth when pressed. Terminal 87 supplies the horn. The horn is earthed to the body at a labelled earth point."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Press the horn button and listen for the relay click. If it clicks, the coil circuit and button work.","If it clicks but the horn is silent, measure the voltage at the horn connector while someone presses the button. If there is 12 V, the horn's earth or the horn itself is faulty.","If there is no click, check the fuse, then measure the voltage at 86 and at 85 while the button is pressed. 12 V at 86 with 12 V at 85 means the earth through the button is missing.","If there is no 12 V at 30, trace back to the fuse and the power feed.","Swap the relay with an identical one if you have any doubt."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"This method, taking the diagram, predicting the voltage at each point, and testing, is the same for every circuit. Over time you will learn the common circuit forms, and a new diagram will look familiar."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Central locking in detail"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A door lock actuator is a small DC motor or a solenoid that moves the lock lever. A **master** actuator at the driver's door often has a switch that tells the module whether the door is locked or unlocked, and the module commands the rest. Some systems have **deadlocks**, which stop the inside handles working when the vehicle is locked from outside. Many have **crash unlock**, where the module unlocks the doors if the airbag sensors detect an impact. A door that unlocks by itself, or one that cannot be locked, may be caused by a faulty door switch, a bad actuator, a wiring break in the door hinge or a failing module. Always disconnect the battery and take care around airbag wiring when you remove a door trim."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Door ajar switch:** tells the module and the interior lamp when a door is open. A stuck switch keeps the lamp on and drains the battery.","**Boot and bonnet switches:** are part of the alarm. A faulty bonnet switch can trigger an alarm and prevent the alarm from setting.","**Child safety locks:** are mechanical or electrical, and stop rear doors being opened from the inside."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Use the diagram for the exact model, year and equipment level. Wiring changes between versions of the same vehicle."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photocopy or print the diagram and mark the tests with a pen as you go. It gives a record of what you checked."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l5", "title": "Body electrical systems", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Comfort and convenience systems use many small motors and relays. Their faults are very common in workshops. This lesson explains how they work and where to look."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Motors and control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most small motors, such as those in windows, mirrors and seats, are **permanent-magnet DC motors**. Reversing the current reverses the direction. This is done with a switch that swaps the power and earth, or two relays, or an electronic module. A module may also sense the motor current to detect an obstruction and stop the motor."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power windows"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Each window has a motor and a regulator (the lifting mechanism), with a switch at the door and a master switch at the driver's door. Faults include a failed switch, worn motor, broken regulator cable, and wiring in the door hinge that has broken from repeated flexing. If one window does not work, the fault is local. If all windows fail, check the fuse, relay and master switch."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Central locking"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A door lock actuator in each door locks and unlocks the door. A control module receives signals from the key, the remote and the door switches. A single door that fails is usually an actuator or a broken wire. If the remote does not work but the key does, check the remote battery first."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wipers, washers and the horn"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Wipers:** a motor drives the blades through a linkage. A park switch inside the motor returns the blades to rest. A timer or rain sensor controls intermittent wipe. Slow or stuck wipers are often a seized linkage or worn motor.","**Washers:** a small pump sends fluid to the jets. Check the fluid level, the hoses and the jets for blockages.","**Horn:** usually works through a relay. A horn that does not work may have a blown fuse, a failed relay or a poor earth. A weak horn sound usually shows a voltage drop."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","First checks"],"rows":[["One window dead","Switch, motor, door wiring"],["All windows dead","Fuse, relay, master switch"],["One door will not lock","Actuator and its wiring"],["Wipers do not park","Park switch in the motor"],["Horn silent","Fuse, relay, horn earth"]],"caption":"Body electrical faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical current draw of body motors"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Motor","Typical running current","If the current is high","If the current is low"],"rows":[["Window motor","About 5 to 12 A","Stiff regulator or a worn channel","Poor connection or a worn brush"],["Door lock actuator","About 2 to 5 A for a moment","Jammed lock mechanism","Worn actuator"],["Wiper motor (low speed)","About 3 to 6 A","Seized linkage, worn bush","Worn brushes, high resistance"],["Mirror motor","About 0.5 to 1.5 A","Frozen mechanism","Worn gear"],["Blower motor (high)","About 10 to 20 A","Failing bearing","Resistor pack or connection problem"]],"caption":"Use these as a guide. Compare with the vehicle's specification."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Measuring the current is the fastest way to decide whether the problem is the motor or the mechanism it drives. High current with slow movement points to a mechanical load. Low current with slow movement points to an electrical resistance problem."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"To tell if a motor is faulty or its supply is faulty, test at the motor plug. If there is correct power and earth and the motor does not turn, replace the motor."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Take care when working in a door: airbag wiring and side-impact sensors may be close to the window and lock parts. Disconnect the battery first and follow the manual."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l6", "title": "Body control, remote keys and immobilisers", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Locks, lights, windows and security used to work with cables and simple switches. On a modern vehicle they are controlled by electronic modules that talk to one another. This lesson explains how the system works, how keys and immobilisers are linked to the engine, and how to approach a fault in them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The body control module"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **body control module** (BCM) is a computer that manages comfort and convenience functions: central locking, lights, wipers, windows, alarm, interior lighting and more. It reads switch signals and sensors, and drives relays and motors. It is linked to other modules on a **CAN bus** or a slower **LIN bus** (module 12). In some designs the BCM is combined with the fuse box. Faults in a BCM may show as several unrelated problems at once. Check its power and earth supply and the network before you suspect the module."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a remote key works"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A **remote key** has a small radio transmitter, usually on 433 MHz or 315 MHz, and a battery, usually a coin cell. When you press a button, it sends a coded signal to a receiver in the vehicle.","The code **rolls**: it changes each time, according to an agreed formula, so a recorded signal cannot be replayed. If the key is pressed too many times out of range, the key and receiver can fall out of step and need resynchronising.","A **keyless entry** (smart key) system uses a low-frequency signal from the vehicle to wake the key when it is within about a metre or two, and the key answers by radio. The vehicle unlocks when you touch the handle. A weak key battery, a nearby source of interference or a damaged antenna can stop it working."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The immobiliser"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **immobiliser** stops the engine from starting unless the right key is present. The key has a small **transponder**, a chip without a battery, that is powered by a coil around the ignition switch. When the key is turned, the coil sends a signal to the transponder, which replies with a code. The immobiliser module checks the code, then agrees with the engine control unit by an exchange of data, and only then does the engine control unit enable the fuel and spark. A rolling or encrypted code makes it very hard to defeat."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Possible cause"],"rows":[["Engine cranks but will not start, security light flashes","The key code was not accepted, a faulty key, a coil or a module fault"],["Engine starts for a second and stops","The engine computer did not receive a valid message from the immobiliser"],["Key works in one car but another key does not","The spare key is not programmed"],["Remote works but the engine will not start","The transponder is faulty or the key was not programmed for the immobiliser"],["Starter does not crank","Starter circuit fault or the immobiliser blocks the starter as well"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnosing a no-start with a security fault"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check that the vehicle's battery is fully charged and that the system voltage is steady. Low voltage causes many false security faults.","Try the spare key. If the spare works, the first key is faulty or the transponder is damaged.","Check the immobiliser warning lamp, which usually flashes or stays on if the system rejects the key.","Read the codes from the body control module, the immobiliser and the engine computer with a scan tool.","Check the wiring and the antenna coil at the ignition switch.","If a module has been replaced, it must be coded or matched to the vehicle and keys."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Programming keys and ownership"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Adding or replacing a key means programming it to the vehicle with a scan tool and, for many vehicles, a security code. This is a security-sensitive job. Before you cut or program a key, check proof of ownership, such as the registration papers and personal identification. Keep a record. Never program a key for a vehicle if you are not sure that the person is the owner. Keys and security data held by a workshop must be kept safe."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How to solder a wire joint"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Cut the damaged section, and strip about 8 to 10 mm of insulation from each end using a proper stripper, without nicking the strands.","Slide a length of adhesive-lined heat-shrink tube over one wire, away from the heat.","Twist the strands together to form a neat joint, or lay them side by side.","Heat the wires with the soldering iron and touch the solder to the wire, not the iron. Let the solder flow through the strands to make a smooth, shiny joint.","Let it cool without moving it. A dull, grainy joint (a cold joint) is poor and must be redone.","Slide the tube over the joint and shrink it with a heat gun, until the adhesive appears at both ends.","Support the wire with a clip or tape so the joint does not bend, and test the circuit."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use rosin-core electrical solder, never acid-core plumbing solder, which corrodes the wire. Avoid overheating the wire, because the insulation can melt back and the strands become brittle."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A security fault looks like an engine fault, but it is not one. If the engine cranks, has fuel and spark commands missing, and the security lamp flashes, test the immobiliser before you take apart the engine."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always ask the customer for all keys when they bring in a vehicle with a security fault, and note the number of keys. A damaged key or a mixed set of keys is a common cause."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l7", "title": "Windows, wipers, mirrors, seats and heated screens in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Comfort systems are full of small motors and switches, and they cause a steady stream of workshop jobs. Each has a typical fault pattern. This lesson explains how the main ones work, how an anti-pinch window protects fingers, how to test a heated rear window and how to approach faults."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power windows"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A window motor drives a **regulator**, either a scissor arm or a cable and pulley, that lifts the glass in a track. A switch gives the motor power in one direction or the other. The simplest systems pass the full motor current through the switch, and the switch contacts wear, burn and fail. More advanced systems use relays or electronic drivers and a module. Many have **one-touch** operation, where a short press makes the window go all the way, and **anti-pinch** protection, where the module senses motor current or a Hall sensor on the motor shaft and reverses the window if something is in the way."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Window slow, then stops","Dry or damaged guide channel, worn regulator or a motor that is weak"],["Window drops into the door, motor runs","Broken cable or clip in the regulator"],["Motor does not run, one switch works","A faulty master switch or wiring break in the door hinge loom"],["One-touch does not work","Needs to be re-initialised after a battery disconnect or glass reset"],["Window goes down but does not rise fully","Anti-pinch triggered because of friction or a need to reset"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"After a battery disconnect or a new regulator, you may need to **initialise** the window by holding the switch up for a few seconds with the glass at the top, as the manual says."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wipers and washers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wiper motor has a worm gear, a crank and a linkage. A **park switch** cam inside the motor keeps the supply on after the switch is turned off until the blades reach the park position. Most wipers have two or more speeds, made with a third brush or a resistor, and an intermittent timer. A **rain sensor** on the windscreen sends infrared light into the glass and measures how much is reflected back. Water on the glass reduces the reflection, so the module starts the wipers. After a windscreen replacement, the sensor's gel pad must be fitted without air bubbles and the system may need to be calibrated."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Wiper arms must be tightened at the right angle of park, and the nut torque must be correct.","A motor that blows its fuse has a jammed linkage or a shorted winding. Free the linkage by hand first.","Washer jets are adjusted with a pin so the spray hits the glass at the right place."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Mirrors and seats"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Electric mirrors have two small motors, one for each axis, in a plastic housing, driven by a joystick switch. Heated mirrors have a thin film on the back of the glass. Folding mirrors have a third motor. Seats have motors for each movement, often with memory modules that store the positions in a database. A seat that will not move at all points to a fuse or supply, and one that does not move in just one direction points to the switch or motor. Seat belt and airbag sensors in the seat need special care, and the seat wiring must be disconnected following the manual's SRS procedure."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Heated rear window and mirrors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The heated rear window is a set of thin horizontal conductive lines printed on the glass. Current flows along each line and warms the glass. It is a big load, often 10 to 20 A, so it is switched by a relay and often by a timer. To test a grid, switch it on and use a voltmeter: connect one lead to the earth side of the grid and run the other along the lines. The voltage should rise evenly from near 0 V at the earth side to 12 V at the supply side. A break shows as a sudden jump in voltage. A broken line can be repaired with a conductive paint kit made for the purpose."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Testing a rear window grid","steps":["Switch on the heater. Connect the black lead of the voltmeter to the earth terminal of the grid.","Touch the red lead to the middle of a heating line. A healthy line reads about 6 V at its middle.","On a line that reads 12 V along its whole length, the break is between the probe point and the earth side. On a line that reads 0 V along its whole length, the break is on the supply side.","Use the point where the reading changes to locate the break within a centimetre or two, and repair it with the conductive paint."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sunroofs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A sunroof uses a motor, cables and a control module. Faults are often dirt in the tracks, a dry cable, or a motor that has lost its learned position. Block drains cause water to enter the cabin. Clean the drain tubes with compressed air at low pressure or a soft wire. Never use a sharp tool that can puncture them."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"For any body motor, measure its current. A high current with weak movement is a mechanical fault, and a low current is an electrical one."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Take care of window glass and regulators that can trap fingers, and disconnect the battery before you take a door trim off. Airbag wiring runs inside many door panels."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l8", "title": "Instruments and accessories", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Instruments tell the driver what the vehicle is doing. Accessories add features. This lesson covers how both work and how to fit accessories without causing problems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gauges and warning lamps"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Traditional gauges have a **sender** (sensor) in the engine or tank and a gauge in the dash that responds to the sender's resistance or voltage. Modern clusters get the data from the ECU over the vehicle network and display it on a screen or with stepper motors. Warning lamps light for faults, such as oil pressure, charging, brakes, airbag, engine management and ABS. Most lamps come on briefly when the ignition is switched on to prove that the bulb works."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel level and temperature senders"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **fuel level sender** is a float arm that moves a wiper over a resistive track, changing its resistance with the fuel level. A **temperature sender** is a thermistor whose resistance changes with temperature. If a gauge reads wrongly, compare the sender's resistance against the manual values and check the earth and wiring."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Audio and aftermarket wiring"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Audio systems need a permanent power supply for memory, a switched supply, an earth and the speaker wires. Use the manufacturer's adaptor leads to avoid cutting the harness. Poor earths cause noise in the speakers. Do not tap into sensor or airbag wiring."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Adding an accessory correctly"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Work out the current: watts ÷ volts. For example, a 120 W pair of auxiliary lamps on 12 V draws 10 A.","Choose a wire that can carry the current with a small drop, and a fuse of about 125% of the load current. Here, a 15 A fuse.","Fit the fuse as close to the power source as possible, so that the wire is protected along its whole length.","Use a relay for any load over about 10 amps, with the switch controlling only the relay coil.","Take the earth to a clean, bare, bolted metal point, and secure and protect all wires from heat and chafing."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Alarms and immobilisers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **immobiliser** stops the engine from starting unless it receives a valid code from the key. An **alarm** sounds if a door, the bonnet or a sensor is triggered. Faults in these systems can cause no-start or random alarms. Use the maker's procedure and scan tool, and do not bypass them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing a fuel level sender"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fuel level","Typical sender resistance","Gauge reading"],"rows":[["Empty","About 250 Ω or 10 Ω, depending on the system","Empty"],["Half","About 100 Ω or 50 Ω","Half"],["Full","About 10 Ω or 250 Ω","Full"]],"caption":"Systems differ, some have high resistance at empty and some at full. Use the values in the manual."},
      {"t":"p","text":"To test a gauge circuit, disconnect the sender wire and earth it through a resistor of the value for half full. The gauge should read half. If it does, the gauge and wiring are good and the sender is at fault. If not, the gauge or wiring is faulty. On a vehicle where the cluster gets its fuel level over the data network, use the scan tool to read the fuel level from the ECU, and compare it with the sender resistance."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speedometer and odometer"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A vehicle speed signal comes from a sensor on the gearbox or from the ABS wheel sensors, and goes to the cluster as pulses or data. If the speedometer fails, or reads wrongly, check the sensor and the wiring. Changing the tyre size or the final drive ratio changes the speedometer reading, because the speed signal comes from rotation, not distance. The odometer is stored in the cluster and often in other modules too. **Altering an odometer is illegal in most countries.** If a cluster is replaced, the mileage must be correctly set following the legal procedure and the change must be recorded."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never connect accessories to airbag, ABS or ECU wiring, and never fit a fuse of a higher rating than the wire can carry. Wrongly fitted accessories cause many vehicle fires."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If an accessory does not work, test the power, the earth and the fuse at the device before you replace it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l9", "title": "Towing electrics and electrical accessories", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Many customers fit tow bars, dash cameras, phone chargers, reversing sensors and extra lights. Done well, these are useful. Done badly, they flatten batteries, set off faults and start fires. This lesson covers the electrical side of towing and the common accessories."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Trailer sockets"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Pin on a 7-pin (12N) socket","Colour (typical)","Function"],"rows":[["1","Yellow","Left indicator"],["2","Blue","Fog lamp (rear)"],["3","White","Earth"],["4","Green","Right indicator"],["5","Brown","Right tail and side lamps"],["6","Red","Brake lamps"],["7","Black","Left tail and side lamps"]],"caption":"Layouts differ by region and by plug type. A 13-pin socket adds a permanent feed, a charging feed, a reversing lamp and a sensing circuit. Always use the pin chart for the plug you are fitting."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fitting towing electrics"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A **vehicle-specific** wiring kit plugs into the rear lamp connectors and the vehicle's module. A **universal** kit taps into the wiring and needs a bypass relay to protect the vehicle's electronics.","Modern vehicles monitor lamp circuits. A trailer adds extra load, so the vehicle needs a module that knows that a trailer is connected. If you tap into the lamp wires without a suitable module, the vehicle may report bulb failures or turn off the lights.","The permanent supply and the charging supply to the trailer need a fuse near the battery, and a fuse in the right size for the wire.","Use sealed connectors, and clip the wires away from the exhaust and moving parts.","Check all lamps and the indicator rate with a trailer or a test box connected.","Some vehicles have towing software that adjusts stability control, lamps, parking sensors and fuel economy. It may need to be activated with a scan tool."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common accessories"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Accessory","What to watch"],"rows":[["Dash camera","Needs a permanent feed for parking mode, with a low-voltage cut-off to protect the battery. Use a fused feed and a proper fuse tap, not a splice."],["Reversing sensors and camera","Power from the reverse lamp circuit. Check the sensor positions and the display. Do not cut wires in a CAN network."],["USB and 12 V chargers","Fit a fuse. A cheap charger can draw current all the time and drain the battery."],["Inverter (12 V to mains)","A 300 W inverter draws about 25 A or more. It needs a thick, short cable and a fuse near the battery."],["Auxiliary lamps","Fit a relay and a fuse, a switch that shows when they are on, and aim them correctly. Check the local law on additional lamps."],["Alarm or tracker","Hidden connections to the vehicle's network are a security weakness. Fit them neatly and test the drain."]]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Sizing an inverter feed","steps":["A 300 W inverter on a 12 V supply at an efficiency of 85% draws 300 ÷ 0.85 ÷ 12 = 29.4 A.","A fuse of 40 A is suitable for this, and a cable of 6 mm² (safe to about 40 A) or larger, kept short to limit voltage drop.","At 29 A the alternator at idle (about 40 A) has little left over, so use the inverter with the engine speed raised, or the battery will be drained."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Accessory checklist"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Decide the load current and choose wire and fuse.","Find a correct power source: a spare fuse slot with a fuse tap, or a relay from the battery.","Find a good earth on clean bare metal.","Run the wire away from heat and sharp edges, using conduit and grommets.","Test the system and the parasitic drain after the job.","Give the customer a note of what was fitted and where the fuse is."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Never splice into data wires, airbag wiring or wiring that you cannot identify. Use the connectors made for the job."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Fit a fuse in every new power wire, close to the supply. A new wire with no fuse is a fire waiting to happen."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m11-l10", "title": "Wiring repair and harnesses", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A wiring repair that is done well lasts the life of the vehicle. A poor one causes faults and can start a fire. This lesson shows how to do it correctly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wire and fuse sizing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Wire size is the cross-section area in mm², or an AWG number. The wire must carry the load current without overheating, with a small voltage drop. Larger current and longer lengths need thicker wire. The fuse is chosen to protect the wire, and should blow before the wire overheats. Use the manual's chart or a reliable wire size table, and use automotive wire with insulation rated for engine bay heat."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Repair methods"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Crimping:** use the right crimp terminal and a proper ratchet crimping tool. A pliers crush crimp is poor. Tug the wire to test the crimp.","**Soldering:** strip, twist, heat the wire and let the solder flow into it. Do not use the soldering iron to heat the solder instead of the wire. Solder alone can be brittle under vibration, so support the wire.","**Heat-shrink:** use adhesive-lined tubing. Slide it on before you join the wire, then shrink it over the joint to seal against water."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Connector repair"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Terminals in connectors can be released with a small tool and replaced. Use the correct terminal type and seal for the connector. Do not cut a connector off and join the wires with a crimp unless the manual allows it. Clean corroded terminals with a contact cleaner, and use dielectric grease on sealed connectors if the maker recommends it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Harness care"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Route wires away from sharp edges, moving parts and hot exhaust parts. Use clips and conduit and keep the original routing. Stagger repairs so they do not form one thick lump. Look for chafing, melted insulation, and rodent or water damage when you inspect a vehicle. Mice and rats chew insulation, and water that enters a connector can travel along the wire under the insulation."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fire risk"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Poor connections heat up under current, and a hot connection can melt insulation and start a fire. Never use household wire, insulation tape alone, or twist-and-tape joints. Never bypass a fuse. If you find melted or burnt wiring, find out what caused the overheating before repairing it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing a crimp and choosing heat-shrink"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Pull test:** after crimping, pull the wire firmly. It must not move or come out. A poor crimp pulls out or twists.","**Visual test:** the insulation grip must hold the insulation, the wire grip must hold the bare wire, and no strands may be outside the barrel.","**Heat-shrink size:** choose a tube that is slightly larger than the joint before shrinking, and shrinks to less than the wire. Use one that has a 3 to 1 shrink ratio and adhesive lining for outside use.","**Rodent damage:** if you find chewed wires, repair them and also find where the animal enters. A nest or food source in the engine bay will bring it back."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Disconnect the battery before you repair wiring. Take extra care near airbag, brake and fuel system wiring. Airbag wiring is usually yellow, and repairs to it should follow the maker's instructions only."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Seal every repair against water. Moisture and vibration are the two main causes of wiring failure."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Trace a circuit, test lamp and horn circuits, and repair a wire","objective":"Trace a circuit on a wiring diagram, test the power and earth of a headlamp and horn circuit, and repair a damaged wire with a crimp or solder joint sealed with heat-shrink.","time":"90 minutes","tools":["Wiring diagram for the training vehicle or board","Digital multimeter and a fused jumper wire","Wire strippers, ratchet crimp tool and crimp connectors, or a soldering iron and solder","Adhesive-lined heat-shrink tubing and a heat gun","Spare wire of the correct size and a notepad"],"safety":["Disconnect the battery before you cut, strip or join any wire.","Handle the soldering iron and heat gun with care, and use a stand when they are hot.","Wear safety glasses when cutting wire and when soldering.","Never bypass a fuse or use a jumper wire without a fuse.","Keep wires away from hot parts and moving parts."],"steps":["Find the headlamp circuit on the wiring diagram. List the fuse, relay, switch, connectors and earth point.","Mark on the diagram where you expect to find power, earth and signal.","Reconnect the battery. With the headlamps on, measure the voltage at the lamp connector and at the fuse.","Measure the voltage drop on the power and earth sides of one lamp.","Find the horn circuit on the diagram and repeat the power, earth and relay checks.","Disconnect the battery. Examine the damaged wire that your instructor has prepared.","Cut out the damage and strip both ends back about 6 mm.","Slide a piece of adhesive-lined heat-shrink onto the wire.","Join the wire with a crimp connector or a solder joint and tug test it.","Slide the heat-shrink over the joint and shrink it until the adhesive shows at the ends.","Secure the wire with a clip so that it does not move or chafe.","Reconnect the battery and test that the circuit works, then record your results."],"checks":["I traced the circuit on the diagram and planned where to test.","I measured power and earth and found the voltage drop on each side.","I made a sound crimp or solder joint and sealed it with heat-shrink.","I disconnected the battery before cutting or joining wires.","I tested the repaired circuit and recorded the result."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m11-q01","q":"Why should you not touch a halogen bulb with bare fingers?","options":["Skin oil makes a hot spot on the glass that shortens the life of the bulb","The glass will absorb oil from the skin and go cloudy at once","The bulb has a coating that is poisonous to touch with the skin","Your fingers could get an electric shock from the glass of the bulb itself"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l1","explain":"Oil burns on the hot glass and causes it to weaken and fail. Handle the bulb by its base or with a clean cloth."},
    {"id":"m11-q02","q":"Why must care be taken when working on HID headlamps?","options":["The bulbs contain mercury that will leak out as a liquid","The bulbs are very hot and burn the skin even when off for hours","The reflector will crack if it is touched with a metal tool","The ballast produces a very high starting voltage"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l1","explain":"The starting voltage can be above 20,000 V. Switch off and disconnect the battery."},
    {"id":"m11-q03","q":"What happens if the headlamps are aimed too high?","options":["They dazzle oncoming drivers","They shorten the life of the bulbs in the housing","They light the road more evenly in front of the vehicle","They use more current than headlamps aimed correctly"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l1","explain":"Aim is checked on level ground against marks or a screen, with the vehicle unloaded."},
    {"id":"m11-q04","q":"What does the bulb code H7 mean?","options":["A dual-filament bayonet tail bulb","A single-filament halogen bulb","A high-intensity xenon discharge lamp","A wedge bulb for number plates"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l2","explain":"H7 is a 55 W single-filament halogen bulb. H4 has two filaments for dipped and main beam in one bulb."},
    {"id":"m11-q05","q":"A 1.0% downward slope is required. The screen is 10 m away and the lamp centre is 65 cm high. Where is the cut-off?","options":["At 75 cm","At 65 cm","At 55 cm","At 10 cm"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l2","explain":"1.0% of 10 m is 10 cm. The cut-off is 10 cm below the lamp centre, so 65 − 10 = 55 cm."},
    {"id":"m11-q06","q":"Why does fitting an LED bulb in a halogen reflector lamp often dazzle other drivers?","options":["The source sits in the wrong place","LED bulbs are always much too bright","LED light cannot be reflected","The lamp becomes hotter than it should"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l2","explain":"A reflector shapes the beam around the position of the filament. A different source scatters the light and spoils the cut-off."},
    {"id":"m11-q07","q":"A vehicle imported from a right-hand traffic country is driven on the left. What is a problem with its headlamps?","options":["The bulbs cannot be changed at all","The lamps are the wrong colour of glass","The headlamps do not have a dipped beam","The beam dazzles oncoming drivers"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l2","explain":"The cut-off is shaped to light the opposite kerb, so the beam rises toward oncoming traffic. The lamps need changing, or an approved correction."},
    {"id":"m11-q08","q":"Why must adaptive or self-levelling lamps be calibrated after repairs?","options":["They must relearn the zero position","The bulbs would not light otherwise","The fuse would blow after the repair","The wipers depend on the lamps"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l2","explain":"Sensors and motors reference a known position. After suspension or lamp work, the module needs to learn it again with a scan tool."},
    {"id":"m11-q09","q":"What is the job of a headlamp relay?","options":["To protect the bulbs from being touched by the driver's hands","To let a small switch current control the heavy headlamp current","To make the headlamps dim gradually when the ignition is turned off","To increase the voltage from the battery for the headlamp bulbs"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"Relays keep the heavy current out of the dashboard switch and wiring."},
    {"id":"m11-q10","q":"On a thermal flasher system, what does very fast flashing usually show?","options":["A failed indicator bulb","A fault in the alternator","A loose rear number plate","A flat battery"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"A missing bulb reduces the current, and the thermal flasher speeds up. Electronic systems vary."},
    {"id":"m11-q11","q":"A brake lamp switch is out of adjustment and the lamps stay on. What is the likely result?","options":["A flat battery and lamps that confuse other drivers","The tyres wear unevenly and the steering pulls to one side","A flat battery, lamps that stay on and brakes that grab on the road","The brakes lock at speed and the engine stalls at traffic lights"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"The lamps draw current while the vehicle is off, and they give a false signal to drivers behind."},
    {"id":"m11-q12","q":"Both lamps on one side fail together. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A failed alternator on that side of the vehicle","A shared fuse, earth or connector","Both bulbs failing at exactly the same moment","A cracked windscreen"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"Shared parts explain two failures at once. A single failed lamp is usually the bulb or socket."},
    {"id":"m11-q13","q":"A vehicle's rear lamps keep blowing fuses. What do you suspect?","options":["A cracked windscreen that has let water into the rear of the car","Low tyre pressure on the rear wheels of the vehicle","A short to earth in the wiring or lamp holder","A weak alternator that is not charging the battery at all"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"Repeated blown fuses mean excess current from a short. Find the cause and do not fit a bigger fuse."},
    {"id":"m11-q14","q":"What should you check first when a single headlamp stops working but the other still works?","options":["The alternator and the battery","The bulb and its socket","The fuel pump relay and its earth","The windscreen wiper fuse"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"One lamp out points to the bulb, socket or its own wiring because the shared parts still work for the other lamp."},
    {"id":"m11-q15","q":"A 21 W bulb is replaced by a 2 W LED. What problem may occur and why?","options":["The bulb will blow at once because it is too bright","The relay will weld shut because of the heat","The flasher stops until the driver switches off","The flasher speeds up: the load is too low"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"A thermal flasher reads a low current as a failed bulb and flashes fast. A load resistor or an LED-compatible flasher fixes it."},
    {"id":"m11-q16","q":"What current does a 21 W bulb draw at 12 V?","options":["0.57 A","252 A","1.75 A","21 A"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l3","explain":"Current = power ÷ voltage = 21 ÷ 12 = 1.75 A."},
    {"id":"m11-q17","q":"What does a wiring diagram show?","options":["The cost of each part in the circuit and the labour time to fit it","The exact location of each part, and the way it is fitted on the vehicle","The service history of each component and the date it was changed","How components are connected electrically, not where they are fitted"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"Use a separate component location guide to find the parts."},
    {"id":"m11-q18","q":"On a wiring diagram, two lines cross with no dot. What does this mean?","options":["The wires carry the same current","The wires are joined at that point","The wires are not connected","The wires are earth connections"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"Connections are shown with a dot at the crossing."},
    {"id":"m11-q19","q":"What do the letters RD/WH on a wiring diagram show?","options":["A relay with a white coil","A rear door switch with a warning horn","A red wire with a white stripe","A reading device with a white lamp"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"The first colour is the base and the second is the stripe."},
    {"id":"m11-q20","q":"Why should you check the connector view on a diagram before testing?","options":["The view tells you the voltage that is present on each wire at the pin","It may show the terminal side or the wire side, which changes the pin positions","The view shows whether the connector is waterproof or not","The view tells you how long each wire is between its ends"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"Reading the wrong view puts the probe on the wrong pin."},
    {"id":"m11-q21","q":"What is the best first step when planning a test from a wiring diagram?","options":["Cut the wire at the first connector so that you can test each half","Remove all of the fuses in the circuit so that it is safe to test","Replace the part at the very end of the circuit and then test again","Mark where power, earth and signal should be when it is working"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"Knowing what to expect lets you recognise what is wrong."},
    {"id":"m11-q22","q":"On a relay, which terminals are normally connected to the coil?","options":["30 and 87","85 and 86","87 and 87a","30 and 86"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"The coil is 85 and 86. The switched contacts are 30 and 87 (or 87a)."},
    {"id":"m11-q23","q":"A horn relay clicks but the horn is silent. 12 V is present at the horn connector when the button is pressed. What is the fault?","options":["The horn button in the steering wheel","The relay coil inside the relay","The fuse in the underbonnet fuse box","The horn's earth or the horn"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l4","explain":"The relay is working and the supply reaches the horn, so the fault is on the earth side or in the horn."},
    {"id":"m11-q24","q":"All the power windows on a vehicle stop working at once. What should you check first?","options":["The regulator in each door, together with its cable and its guides","The fuse, the relay and the master switch","The windscreen wiper motor, since it shares the same fuse box","Each window motor in turn, starting with the one at the driver's door"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l5","explain":"A common failure point affects all windows. Individual faults affect one."},
    {"id":"m11-q25","q":"A window works from the master switch but not from its own door switch. What is likely?","options":["A faulty door switch or its wiring","A failed window motor that has worn out its brushes and its gears","A dead battery that has too little charge to move the glass","A blown main fuse for all of the windows on the vehicle"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l5","explain":"The motor works, so the fault is in the switch or its wiring."},
    {"id":"m11-q26","q":"Why do wipers fail to park properly?","options":["The windscreen is dirty and is making the blades drag on the glass","The wiper blades are the wrong size for the windscreen","The park switch in the motor is worn or faulty","The washer pump has failed and will not send fluid to the jets"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l5","explain":"The park switch keeps the motor running until the blades reach the rest position."},
    {"id":"m11-q27","q":"A horn sounds weak. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The horn has been fitted upside down on its bracket in the bumper","The battery is overcharged and is supplying too high a voltage","Low voltage at the horn because of a voltage drop in the wiring or earth","The alternator belt is too tight and is slowing the engine down"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l5","explain":"A weak horn is a clue to resistance in the circuit. Do a voltage-drop test."},
    {"id":"m11-q28","q":"A window motor runs slowly and draws a high current. What does this suggest?","options":["A poor connection in the supply wire","A blown fuse in the circuit","A flat spare wheel","A stiff regulator or binding channel"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l5","explain":"A high current with slow movement means a mechanical load. A low current with slow movement means a resistance in the supply or earth path."},
    {"id":"m11-q29","q":"What does a transponder chip in a key do?","options":["It opens the fuel flap by remote control","It answers the immobiliser with a code","It stores the radio stations and clock","It starts the engine by itself in the cold"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l6","explain":"The chip, powered by a coil round the ignition switch, replies with a code. The immobiliser checks it before the engine can start."},
    {"id":"m11-q30","q":"What is a rolling code?","options":["A code that stays the same for every key","A code printed on the key blade","A code that changes with each use","A code that rolls through all the radio stations"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l6","explain":"Each press uses a new value from an agreed sequence. A thief who records one signal cannot use it again."},
    {"id":"m11-q31","q":"The engine cranks, but will not start, and the security lamp flashes. What should you test first?","options":["The key code and the immobiliser","The compression of each cylinder in turn","The brake fluid level in the reservoir","The wheel alignment on the front axle"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l6","explain":"A flashing security light with a crank and no start points to the immobiliser rejecting the key. Test it before you strip an engine."},
    {"id":"m11-q32","q":"What should you check before programming a new key?","options":["The weather forecast for the day","Proof that they own the vehicle","The colour of the vehicle paint","The tyre tread depth"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l6","explain":"Key programming is security-sensitive. Verify ownership with papers and identification, and keep a record."},
    {"id":"m11-q33","q":"How does an anti-pinch window sense an obstruction?","options":["A rise in motor current","It uses a camera in the door","It measures the weather outside","It feels the temperature of the glass"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l7","explain":"The module watches motor current or a Hall sensor on the motor. A sudden rise or slowing means a blockage, and it reverses the window."},
    {"id":"m11-q34","q":"On a heated rear window grid, a line reads 12 V along its whole length. Where is the break?","options":["On the supply side of the line","In the middle of the line only","On the earth side of the line","There is no break"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l7","explain":"A line that stays at supply voltage along its length is cut between the probe and the earth end, so no current flows to earth."},
    {"id":"m11-q35","q":"Why might the one-touch window feature stop working after a battery disconnect?","options":["The glass shrank in the cold","The window motor is now polarised","The regulator has gone into the door","It lost its learned limits"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l7","explain":"The module learns the top and bottom positions. After a disconnect, it needs initialising, as the manual describes."},
    {"id":"m11-q36","q":"A wiper motor draws very high current and the blades are stuck. What is the likely cause?","options":["A jammed linkage","A flat spare tyre in the boot","A low washer fluid level in the bottle","A blown brake bulb at the rear"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l7","explain":"A motor that is stalled draws a high current. Free the linkage and check the pivots before replacing the motor."},
    {"id":"m11-q37","q":"What is a fuel level sender?","options":["A sensor that measures the fuel pressure in the rail at the engine","A valve that stops the tank from overflowing when it is being filled","A pump that sends fuel from the tank to the engine at pressure","A float arm that moves over a resistive track"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"The resistance of the track changes with the float position, and the gauge reads that resistance as the fuel level."},
    {"id":"m11-q38","q":"What current does a 120 W lamp set draw on a 12 V system?","options":["10 A","1,440 A","0.1 A","1 A"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"Current = power ÷ voltage, so I = 120 ÷ 12 = 10 A. Knowing the current lets you choose the right wire size and fuse."},
    {"id":"m11-q39","q":"What is a suitable fuse for a 10 A accessory load?","options":["About 100 A, which is ten times as large as the load current","About 1 A, which is a tenth of the load that the lamps draw","About 15 A, which is roughly 125% of the load","No fuse is needed at all, because the wire is already thick"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"The fuse should protect the wire without blowing in normal use."},
    {"id":"m11-q40","q":"Where should the fuse for an added accessory be fitted?","options":["Inside the passenger cabin only, where it can be reached easily","As close to the accessory as possible, at the far end of the wire","In the middle of the wire, so both ends are the same length","As close to the power source as possible"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"This protects the whole length of the wire from a short."},
    {"id":"m11-q41","q":"When should a relay be used when fitting an accessory?","options":["Only on diesel vehicles, because petrol vehicles have no need of them","Never, because relays always cause faults in a vehicle circuit and fail early","Only for loads under 1 amp, because bigger loads damage the relay","For loads over about 10 amps, so the switch carries only the coil current"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"The relay keeps the heavy current out of the switch and thin wiring."},
    {"id":"m11-q42","q":"Why must you never connect an accessory to airbag wiring?","options":["The airbag wiring is always live at 48 V and will burn the relay","It could affect a safety system and cause it to fail or fire unexpectedly","The wiring is too thin to carry the current that a relay would need","The airbag wiring has no earth and so the relay cannot work"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"Safety system circuits must never be altered."},
    {"id":"m11-q43","q":"To test a fuel gauge circuit, you earth the sender wire through a half-full resistor. The gauge reads half. What is faulty?","options":["The gauge","The sender","The wiring","The fuse"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"If the gauge and wiring give the right reading with a known resistance, then the sender must be the faulty part."},
    {"id":"m11-q44","q":"Why does changing to larger tyres give a wrong speedometer reading?","options":["A larger tyre covers more road per turn","The tyres change the speed of light in the cabin","The speedometer is driven by the fuel gauge","Larger tyres raise the engine speed itself"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l8","explain":"The speed signal comes from rotation. A larger tyre covers more road per rotation, so the car goes faster than the display shows."},
    {"id":"m11-q45","q":"What does a vehicle-specific towing kit do that a universal kit does not?","options":["It makes the trailer lighter","It charges the trailer battery faster","It plugs into the vehicle's modules","It removes the need for a socket"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l9","explain":"A specific kit uses the right connectors and signals for the vehicle. A universal kit taps wires and usually needs a bypass relay."},
    {"id":"m11-q46","q":"A 300 W inverter at 85% efficiency on 12 V draws about what current?","options":["About 3 A","About 25 W","About 3,600 A","About 29 A"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l9","explain":"Current = 300 ÷ 0.85 ÷ 12 = 29.4 A. It needs a thick, short cable and a fuse near the battery."},
    {"id":"m11-q47","q":"Why may a car show bulb failure warnings after a trailer kit is tapped into the lamps?","options":["The trailer lights make the bulbs too bright","The module sees a changed load on the lamp circuit","The warning lights are always switched on at random","The engine is overheating"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l9","explain":"Lamp circuits are monitored. Wiring a trailer in parallel changes the current the module expects."},
    {"id":"m11-q48","q":"Where should a fuse be fitted in a new power wire?","options":["Next to the accessory itself","Anywhere along the wire","Close to the power source","On the earth wire"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l9","explain":"The fuse protects the wire between it and the supply. Placing it close to the source protects the whole length."},
    {"id":"m11-q49","q":"Why is a pliers crush crimp a poor wire repair?","options":["It makes a weak joint that can pull out or corrode","It uses too much solder to hold the wire, which makes it brittle","It makes the joint hotter than a ratchet crimp does in use","It makes the wire too stiff to bend round the harness clips"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"A ratchet crimp tool makes a gas-tight, strong connection."},
    {"id":"m11-q50","q":"What is the purpose of adhesive-lined heat-shrink?","options":["To increase the current rating of the wire","To seal the joint against water","To reduce the resistance of the joint","To insulate against high voltage only"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"When heated, the adhesive melts and seals the ends."},
    {"id":"m11-q51","q":"Why should repairs on neighbouring wires be staggered?","options":["So that the current is shared between the wires in the harness","So they do not form a thick lump that can chafe and trap moisture","So that the fuse blows more easily when there is a short in it","So that the wires all have different lengths in the loom afterwards"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"A neat harness is easier to tape, route and inspect."},
    {"id":"m11-q52","q":"What should you do before repairing wiring?","options":["Disconnect the battery","Start the engine","Switch on all the lights","Remove the dashboard"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"A short circuit during repair can cause sparks, damage or a fire."},
    {"id":"m11-q53","q":"What colour is airbag wiring usually?","options":["Green, and it can be repaired with any crimp that you have in the box","Red, and it is the main battery cable to the starter motor","Yellow, and it must only be repaired by the maker's procedure","Black, and it carries only a small 5 V signal from the sensor"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"Airbag circuits should never be tested or altered casually."},
    {"id":"m11-q54","q":"Why is tape alone not a good wire joint?","options":["Tape is too expensive to use on vehicles, and so it is never fitted","Tape is illegal on all vehicles and the garage can be fined for it","Tape makes the wire too thick to route through the harness clips","It does not make an electrical or waterproof joint and it can slip"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"A proper joint is crimped or soldered, then sealed."},
    {"id":"m11-q55","q":"How can you check a crimped wire terminal?","options":["Smell the terminal for signs of burning","Heat it with a lighter for ten seconds","Tap it with a hammer to test its grip","Pull it. A good crimp does not move"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"A pull test shows a poor crimp straight away. A good crimp grips both the insulation and the wire."},
    {"id":"m11-q56","q":"Why is adhesive-lined heat-shrink used on an outdoor wire repair?","options":["It seals the joint against water","It makes the wire thinner","It is the cheapest way to join a wire","It makes the wire change colour"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m11-l10","explain":"The adhesive melts as the tube shrinks and seals both ends. This stops water entering the joint."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m11-c01","front":"Why not touch a halogen bulb with your fingers?","back":"Skin oil creates a hot spot and the bulb fails early.","lesson":"m11-l1"},
    {"id":"m11-c02","front":"Why must you be careful with HID lamps?","back":"The ballast makes a starting voltage over 20,000 V.","lesson":"m11-l1"},
    {"id":"m11-c03","front":"What is dipped beam?","back":"Low beam that lights the road without dazzling others.","lesson":"m11-l1"},
    {"id":"m11-c04","front":"What happens if headlamps are aimed too high?","back":"They dazzle oncoming drivers.","lesson":"m11-l1"},
    {"id":"m11-c05","front":"What do H4, H7 and H11 mean?","back":"Halogen bulb codes. H4 is dual filament (60/55 W). H7 and H11 are single filament, 55 W.","lesson":"m11-l2"},
    {"id":"m11-c06","front":"How do you set a 1.0% cut-off at 10 m?","back":"The cut-off is 10 cm below the lamp centre height on the screen.","lesson":"m11-l2"},
    {"id":"m11-c07","front":"Why do LED bulbs in halogen lamps dazzle?","back":"The light source is in the wrong place for the reflector, so the beam scatters.","lesson":"m11-l2"},
    {"id":"m11-c08","front":"What does a headlamp relay do?","back":"It lets a small switch current control the heavy lamp current.","lesson":"m11-l3"},
    {"id":"m11-c09","front":"What does fast indicator flash often mean?","back":"A failed bulb, on thermal flasher systems.","lesson":"m11-l3"},
    {"id":"m11-c10","front":"What does the brake lamp switch do?","back":"It closes when the pedal moves and lights the brake lamps.","lesson":"m11-l3"},
    {"id":"m11-c11","front":"What causes repeated blown fuses?","back":"A short circuit or an overload. Find the cause.","lesson":"m11-l3"},
    {"id":"m11-c12","front":"Why do LED indicator bulbs flash fast?","back":"The load is too low for the flasher. Use a load resistor or an LED-compatible flasher.","lesson":"m11-l3"},
    {"id":"m11-c13","front":"What does a wiring diagram show?","back":"How components are connected, not where they are on the vehicle.","lesson":"m11-l4"},
    {"id":"m11-c14","front":"What does a dot at a line crossing mean?","back":"The wires are connected at that point.","lesson":"m11-l4"},
    {"id":"m11-c15","front":"What does RD/WH mean?","back":"A red wire with a white stripe.","lesson":"m11-l4"},
    {"id":"m11-c16","front":"What should you do before planning tests?","back":"Mark where power, earth and signal should be when working.","lesson":"m11-l4"},
    {"id":"m11-c17","front":"How do you trace a horn circuit fault?","back":"Listen for the relay, check voltage at the horn, check the earth, then the relay coil feeds and the fuse.","lesson":"m11-l4"},
    {"id":"m11-c18","front":"What if all the windows stop at once?","back":"Check the fuse, relay and master switch.","lesson":"m11-l5"},
    {"id":"m11-c19","front":"What does a weak horn suggest?","back":"A voltage drop in the supply or earth.","lesson":"m11-l5"},
    {"id":"m11-c20","front":"What does a wiper park switch do?","back":"It keeps the motor running until the blades are at rest.","lesson":"m11-l5"},
    {"id":"m11-c21","front":"Window motor: high current and slow?","back":"A mechanical problem: stiff regulator or channel. Low current and slow suggests an electrical resistance.","lesson":"m11-l5"},
    {"id":"m11-c22","front":"What is a rolling code?","back":"A key code that changes every use so a recording cannot be replayed.","lesson":"m11-l6"},
    {"id":"m11-c23","front":"What does an immobiliser transponder do?","back":"Replies to the immobiliser with a code. The engine only runs if the code is accepted.","lesson":"m11-l6"},
    {"id":"m11-c24","front":"Before programming a key, what must you do?","back":"Check proof of ownership and keep a record.","lesson":"m11-l6"},
    {"id":"m11-c25","front":"How is a rear window grid tested?","back":"Run a voltmeter along each line from the earth side. A sudden voltage jump shows the break.","lesson":"m11-l7"},
    {"id":"m11-c26","front":"What is initialising a window?","back":"Teaching the module the top and bottom positions again after a battery disconnect or repair.","lesson":"m11-l7"},
    {"id":"m11-c27","front":"What does a rain sensor measure?","back":"How much infrared light is reflected from the glass. Water reduces the reflection.","lesson":"m11-l7"},
    {"id":"m11-c28","front":"What is a fuel level sender?","back":"A float arm moving over a resistive track.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c29","front":"How do you find the current from the power?","back":"I = P ÷ V.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c30","front":"What fuse rating suits a 10 A load?","back":"About 15 A, roughly 125% of the load.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c31","front":"Where is the fuse for an added accessory fitted?","back":"As close to the power source as possible.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c32","front":"When is a relay needed?","back":"For loads over about 10 A, so the switch carries only the coil current.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c33","front":"What is an immobiliser?","back":"A system that stops the engine starting without a valid key code.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c34","front":"How do you check a fuel gauge circuit?","back":"Earth the sender wire through a known resistor and see whether the gauge reads correctly.","lesson":"m11-l8"},
    {"id":"m11-c35","front":"7-pin (12N) trailer earth pin?","back":"Pin 3, usually white.","lesson":"m11-l9"},
    {"id":"m11-c36","front":"Why must towing kits protect the vehicle's lamp monitoring?","back":"A trailer changes the lamp circuit load, so a bypass relay or module is needed.","lesson":"m11-l9"},
    {"id":"m11-c37","front":"How do you size a feed for an inverter?","back":"Current = power ÷ efficiency ÷ voltage. Choose a short thick cable and a fuse near the battery.","lesson":"m11-l9"},
    {"id":"m11-c38","front":"What is a good wire repair?","back":"Crimp or solder, sealed with adhesive-lined heat-shrink and clipped.","lesson":"m11-l10"},
    {"id":"m11-c39","front":"Why not use a pliers crush crimp?","back":"It makes a weak joint that can pull out or corrode.","lesson":"m11-l10"},
    {"id":"m11-c40","front":"What is wire stagger?","back":"Repairing neighbouring wires at different points to avoid a thick lump.","lesson":"m11-l10"},
    {"id":"m11-c41","front":"What colour is airbag wiring?","back":"Usually yellow.","lesson":"m11-l10"},
    {"id":"m11-c42","front":"What must you do before wiring repairs?","back":"Disconnect the battery.","lesson":"m11-l10"},
    {"id":"m11-c43","front":"How do you test a crimp?","back":"Pull the wire firmly. It must not move or twist. Check that the wire and insulation grips are correct.","lesson":"m11-l10"},
    {"id":"m11-c44","front":"Why use adhesive-lined heat-shrink?","back":"It seals against water, which is a main cause of wiring failure.","lesson":"m11-l10"}
  ]
});
